<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castulo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castulo was an ancient Iberian city in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linares">Linares</a>, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the chief town of the Oretani. It controlled rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lead mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himilce">Himilce</a> was said to come from there. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it became a municipium and minted coins, and it declined in late antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castulo.jpg" alt="Castulo" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miccionis (relief signature) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TS_Hisp%C3%A1nica_Hayes_132_de_C%C3%A1stulo_(M.A.N._1969-33-1)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
